.SH NAME
.PP
\f[B]mana\f[R] \[en] MANA family of commands for checkpointing MPI jobs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]mana_coordinator\f[R] [--help] [--verbose]
[\f[I]DMTCP_OPTIONS\f[R]]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]mana_launch\f[R] [--help] [--verbose] [--timing] [--gdb] [--ckptdir
\f[I]DIR\f[R]] [\f[I]DMTCP_OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[I]PROGRAM\f[R]
[\f[I]ARGS\&...\f[R]]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]mana_status\f[R] [--help] [--verbose] [\f[I]COMMAND\f[R]]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]mana_restart\f[R] [--help] [--verbose] [--timing] [--gdb]
[--restartdir \f[I]DIR\f[R]] [--ckptdir \f[I]DIR\f[R]]
[\f[I]DMTCP_OPTIONS\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\f[B]MANA\f[R] is a package that enables checkpoint-restart for MPI
jobs.
The name MANA stands for \[lq]MPI-Agnostic Network-Agnostic
Checkpointing\[rq].
It is designed to be compatible with most MPI implementations and most
underlying networks.
MANA is built on top of the DMTCP checkpointing package.
.PP
A MANA job uses four commands.
\f[C]mana_coordinator\f[R] starts the coordinator that the ranks connect
to.
\f[C]mana_launch\f[R] runs each rank of an MPI program under MANA.
\f[C]mana_status\f[R] lists the ranks and asks the coordinator for a
checkpoint.
\f[C]mana_restart\f[R] restarts the ranks from their checkpoint.
The MPI program needs no change and no relinking.
The four commands are wrappers of DMTCP\[cq]s commands, and they accept
DMTCP\[cq]s options too (see COMMANDS AND OPTIONS).
.PP
MANA\[cq]s website, <https://mpickpt.github.io/mana/>, has the rest of
MANA\[cq]s documentation: a user guide, a developer guide and the
release notes.
.SH CHECKPOINT AND RESTART
.PP
For example, with the example program \f[C]examples/ring\f[R] (see
BUILDING) on one host, from MANA\[cq]s directory:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
bin/mana_coordinator                         # 1. Start a coordinator.
mpirun -np 4 bin/mana_launch examples/ring   # 2. Run the program under MANA.
bin/mana_status --bcheckpoint                # 3. In another shell: checkpoint.
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
At step 3, each rank N writes its checkpoint into the directory
\f[C]ckpt_rank_N\f[R] in the current directory.
The command returns when the checkpoint is complete, and the program
goes on.
To stop the job and restart it from the checkpoint:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# 4. Stop the job, for example with Ctrl-C.
bin/mana_coordinator                         # 5. Start a new coordinator.
mpirun -np 4 bin/mana_restart                # 6. Restart from ckpt_rank_*.
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The restarted program goes on from where the checkpoint was taken.
Restart with the same number of ranks.
A coordinator exits when its last rank exits, so each run needs a new
one.
.PP
Other ways to checkpoint:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]bin/mana_status --kcheckpoint\f[R]: checkpoint, then stop the job
(steps 3 and 4 in one command).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]bin/mana_status --checkpoint\f[R]: checkpoint, and return at once,
before the checkpoint is done.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]bin/mana_coordinator -i SECONDS\f[R]: checkpoint every
\f[I]SECONDS\f[R] seconds.
.IP \[bu] 2
From the program, with DMTCP\[cq]s API (see the directory
\f[C]dmtcp/test/plugin/applic-initiated-ckpt\f[R]).
.PP
A new checkpoint replaces the previous one.
\f[C]mana_launch\f[R] stops if the checkpoint directory already has
\f[C]ckpt_rank_*\f[R] directories: move or delete an old checkpoint
before a new run.
.PP
With Slurm, run the same commands in an allocation, with \f[C]srun\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
salloc -N 2 -t 01:00:00  # or your cluster\[aq]s command for an allocation
bin/mana_coordinator -i 60
srun -N 2 bin/mana_launch examples/ring
bin/mana_coordinator -i 60
srun -N 2 bin/mana_restart
\f[R]
.fi
.SH COMMANDS AND OPTIONS
.PP
Each MANA command is a wrapper of a DMTCP command:
\f[C]mana_coordinator\f[R] runs \f[C]dmtcp_coordinator\f[R],
\f[C]mana_launch\f[R] runs \f[C]dmtcp_launch\f[R], \f[C]mana_status\f[R]
runs \f[C]dmtcp_command\f[R], and \f[C]mana_restart\f[R] runs the
restart of \f[C]dmtcp_restart\f[R] in MANA\[cq]s lower half.
A MANA command handles the options below, and passes the others to its
DMTCP command, so DMTCP\[cq]s options work too.
MANA sets some of them itself: \f[C]--exit-on-last\f[R] and
\f[C]--daemon\f[R] for \f[C]dmtcp_coordinator\f[R], and
\f[C]--join-coordinator\f[R] and \f[C]--no-gzip\f[R] for
\f[C]dmtcp_launch\f[R].
For DMTCP\[cq]s options, see each DMTCP command\[cq]s \f[C]--help\f[R],
DMTCP\[cq]s man pages (see SEE ALSO), and DMTCP\[cq]s documentation at
<https://dmtcp.github.io/>.
.SS mana_coordinator
.PP
Starts a DMTCP coordinator in the background, and writes its host and
port to a file where the other commands find it (see FILES).
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]-i SECONDS\f[R]\f[B]\f[R], \f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--interval SECONDS\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Checkpoint every \f[I]SECONDS\f[R] seconds (default: 0, no periodic
checkpoints).
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--verbose\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Print the \f[C]dmtcp_coordinator\f[R] command that
\f[C]mana_coordinator\f[R] runs.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--help\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Print the options of \f[C]dmtcp_coordinator\f[R].
.SS mana_launch
.PP
Runs \f[I]PROGRAM\f[R] with \f[I]ARGS\f[R] under MANA.
Run \f[C]mana_launch\f[R] under the MPI launcher, such as \f[C]srun\f[R]
or \f[C]mpirun -np N\f[R], in place of \f[I]PROGRAM\f[R].
A coordinator must be running.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--ckptdir DIR\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Write the checkpoints into \f[I]DIR\f[R], which must exist (default: the
current directory).
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--timing\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Rank 0 prints to stderr the time since the start at each step of a
checkpoint, and at exit.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--verbose\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Print the \f[C]dmtcp_launch\f[R] command that \f[C]mana_launch\f[R]
runs, and DMTCP\[cq]s notes and warnings, which are otherwise not
printed.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--gdb\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Run the \f[C]dmtcp_launch\f[R] command under \f[C]gdb --args\f[R], to
debug MANA itself.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--help\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Print a usage statement.
.PP
\f[C]mana_launch\f[R] loads the plugins of DMTCP\[cq]s
\f[C]--with-plugin\f[R] after MANA\[cq]s own.
.SS mana_status
.PP
Without \f[I]COMMAND\f[R], lists the ranks that are connected to the
coordinator.
A \f[I]COMMAND\f[R] asks the coordinator for an action:
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--checkpoint\f[R]\f[B]\f[R], \f[B]\f[R]\f[C]-c\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Checkpoint the job.
The job goes on.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--bcheckpoint\f[R]\f[B]\f[R], \f[B]\f[R]\f[C]-bc\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Checkpoint the job, and return when the checkpoint is done.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--kcheckpoint\f[R]\f[B]\f[R], \f[B]\f[R]\f[C]-kc\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Checkpoint the job, then stop it.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]-i SECONDS\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Checkpoint every \f[I]SECONDS\f[R] seconds from now on (0: no periodic
checkpoints).
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--verbose\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Print the \f[C]dmtcp_command\f[R] command that \f[C]mana_status\f[R]
runs.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--help\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Print the usage of \f[C]dmtcp_command\f[R], which lists the other
commands.
.SS mana_restart
.PP
Restarts a job from its checkpoint.
Run \f[C]mana_restart\f[R] under the MPI launcher, with the same number
of ranks as the job, after a new \f[C]mana_coordinator\f[R].
Each rank N restarts from \f[C]ckpt_rank_N\f[R], and keeps the
\f[C]MANA_P2P_WAIT\f[R] mode of the job.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--restartdir DIR\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Restart from the \f[C]ckpt_rank_*\f[R] directories in \f[I]DIR\f[R]
(default: the current directory).
\f[C]mana_restart\f[R] stops if a checkpoint there is incomplete (a
\f[C].temp\f[R] file).
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--ckptdir DIR\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Write later checkpoints into \f[I]DIR\f[R], which must exist.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--timing\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Rank 0 prints to stderr the time since the restart at the restart, at
each step of a checkpoint, and at exit.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--verbose\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Print the command that \f[C]mana_restart\f[R] runs, and DMTCP\[cq]s
notes and warnings, which are otherwise not printed.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--gdb\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Run the restart under \f[C]gdb --args\f[R], to debug MANA itself.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]--help\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
Print a usage statement.
.SH BUILDING
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
git clone https://github.com/mpickpt/mana
cd mana
\&./configure
make -j
make tests  # the tests, and the example examples/ring
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[C]make\f[R] puts MANA\[cq]s commands in \f[C]bin/\f[R].
To install MANA into another directory, run
\f[C]./configure --prefix=DIR\f[R], \f[C]make\f[R] and
\f[C]make install\f[R].
The commands are then in \f[C]DIR/bin\f[R].
.SH NOTES
.PP
MANA supports most features of DMTCP, including plugins
(\f[C]mana_launch --with-plugin\f[R]) and the modification of
environment variables at restart (\f[C]dmtcp/plugin/modify-env\f[R]).
.PP
MANA does not support DMTCP\[cq]s distinct checkpoint filenames
(\f[C]--enable-unique-checkpoint-filenames\f[R]): \f[C]mana_restart\f[R]
takes one image from each \f[C]ckpt_rank_N\f[R] directory, and not
always the newest.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]MANA_P2P_WAIT\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
How \f[C]MPI_Send\f[R] and \f[C]MPI_Recv\f[R] wait for their message.
\f[C]polling\f[R] (the default): as \f[C]MPI_Isend\f[R] or
\f[C]MPI_Irecv\f[R], then a loop of \f[C]MPI_Test\f[R] in MANA.
\f[C]blocking\f[R]: in the MPI library\[cq]s own \f[C]MPI_Send\f[R] and
\f[C]MPI_Recv\f[R].
Both modes checkpoint and restart the same way.
\f[C]polling\f[R] can be faster when the MPI library\[cq]s blocking wait
yields the processor (MPICH 5).
\f[C]blocking\f[R] is faster on Cray MPICH, and much faster when two
ranks share a core.
In \f[C]blocking\f[R] mode, MANA initializes the MPI library with
\f[C]MPI_THREAD_MULTIPLE\f[R]; with a library that does not provide it,
\f[C]blocking\f[R] can fail at a checkpoint (e.g., over UCX).
MANA reads the variable at \f[C]MPI_Init\f[R] and keeps the mode after
restart.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]MANA_PRELOAD\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
A colon-separated list of libraries that \f[C]mana_launch\f[R] loads
ahead of MANA, so that they intercept MPI calls before MANA does (e.g.,
a PMPI profiler).
A library in \f[C]LD_PRELOAD\f[R] loads after them.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]DMTCP_LOG_LEVEL\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
How much DMTCP prints to stderr: \f[C]error\f[R], \f[C]warn\f[R],
\f[C]note\f[R] or \f[C]trace\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]DMTCP_RESTART_PAUSE\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
DMTCP/MANA will pause during restart, at the stage given by the value (1
to 7), to allow \f[C]gdb attach\f[R] (GDB must be on same node.)
.SH DEBUGGING
.PP
To see status of ranks (especially during checkpoint), try:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
bin/mana_status
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To inspect a checkpoint image from, for example, Rank \f[C]0\f[R], try:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dmtcp/util/readdmtcp.sh ckpt_rank_0/ckpt_*.dmtcp
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To debug during restart at stage `N', replace `N' below, and execute:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
srun ... env DMTCP_RESTART_PAUSE=N bin/mana_restart &
gdb -p PID  # PID of the paused rank, from another terminal
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To see the stack, you then may or may not need to try some of the
following:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
(gdb) source dmtcp/util/gdb-dmtcp-utils.py
(gdb) load-symbols-library ADDRESS-OR-LIBRARY-SUBSTRING
(gdb) dmtcp  # to see other available commands
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
If you are debugging the lower half internals of MANA, you may need:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
(gdb) file bin/lower-half
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
If debugging the low-level restart process, please read
src/lower-half/README.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]\[ti]/.mana.rc\f[R]\f[B]\f[R], or \f[B]\f[R]\f[C]\[ti]/.mana-slurm-JOBID.rc\f[R]\f[B]\f[R] in a Slurm job
The coordinator\[cq]s host and port, written by
\f[C]mana_coordinator\f[R] and read by the other commands.
\f[C]mana_launch\f[R] and \f[C]mana_restart\f[R] delete these files when
they are more than a week old.
.TP
\f[B]\f[R]\f[C]ckpt_rank_N/\f[R]\f[B]\f[R]
The checkpoint of rank N, in the checkpoint directory: its image
\f[C]ckpt_*.dmtcp\f[R] and the file \f[C]header.mana\f[R].
